WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
Where preference tuning fits.

2
00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:11.309
Everything you trained in week 3 was supervised fine-tuning: show the model inputs and the exact outputs you want, and minimize the loss on those outputs.

3
00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:15.728
That works when you can write down the right answer, as you can for extraction.

4
00:00:16.008 --> 00:00:17.629
For many behaviors you can’t.

5
00:00:17.909 --> 00:00:27.189
There are a hundred acceptable ways to write a helpful support reply, and the difference between a good one and a great one is easier to recognize than to produce.

6
00:00:27.469 --> 00:00:31.821
People are much better at comparing two answers than at writing the ideal one.

7
00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:39.312
Preference tuning turns comparisons into training signal: for a prompt, here are two responses, and this one is better.

8
00:00:39.592 --> 00:00:45.629
It is the stage that turned instruction-following base models into the assistants you use every day.

9
00:00:45.909 --> 00:00:47.595
This lesson is conceptual.

10
00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:54.744
You won’t run preference tuning in this studio’s project, because your extraction task has gold labels and doesn’t need it.

11
00:00:55.024 --> 00:01:01.104
But you should know what it is, how its main methods work, and how to recognize a team problem that calls for it.

12
00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:03.411
Two other ideas round out the lesson.

13
00:01:03.691 --> 00:01:08.299
AI feedback replaces some human comparisons with a model’s judgments.

14
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:19.608
Distillation trains a small model to imitate a large one, which is often the cheapest way to build the fine-tuning dataset you used in weeks 2 and 3, and comes with traps worth knowing.
